---
/**
 * foodcraft / HeaderBO — THE STICKY BLUSH RAIL. "Sipsip Tea Bar"'s front gate,
 * reproduced from the design's own <header>: a 94%-opaque --paper bar over a
 * 14px backdrop blur, and one flex row at 16px top and bottom that spreads two
 * things 20px apart — the brand lock (a 36px purple disc holding the shop's
 * initial, then a 23px Baloo 2 wordmark with a 9px/.28em purple town tag
 * stacked under it) and a wrapping row of 13px nav pills at a 6px gap.
 *
 * ROLE: wayfinding, and nothing else. This design puts NO action in its header
 * — see decision 1.
 *
 * EDITABLE HOOKS — every visible string on this rail, and nothing else:
 *   footer.brand                     — the wordmark (also the footer's; one
 *                                      field, two places, on purpose)
 *   footer.locality                  — the purple town tag under it
 *   navbar_links.${i}.label          — one nav pill's label
 *   navbar_links.${i}.href           — that pill's target (data-href-field)
 * All four are declared in components/editor/genericContentSchema.ts under the
 * `header` group, so every one is a hook a sidebar input actually owns. A
 * data-field on any other path would be a dead hook nothing can edit.
 *
 * DERIVED, never authored here:
 *   - the wordmark falls back to layout.businessName when content.footer.brand
 *     is blank, because that is the same name from the same submission.
 *   - THE DISC'S LETTER. The design's circle holds `{{ initial }}` — the first
 *     character of the shop name, upper-cased. It is derived from the wordmark
 *     the owner supplied, never chosen here, and it disappears with the
 *     wordmark. Array.from() rather than charAt() so a name opening on an
 *     astral character (an emoji, some scripts) yields that whole character
 *     instead of half a surrogate pair.
 *   - every in-page href is resolved by onPage() against the ids PageBO really
 *     renders (see the anchor guard below).
 *   - `i` is the ORIGINAL, pre-filter index of a nav link, so dropping an
 *     unreachable row 2 cannot misdirect an edit into row 3.
 *
 * NOT RENDERED, deliberately:
 *   - THE DESIGN'S OWN COPY. Not one word of the designer's demo text is in
 *     this file. A tea bar whose owner supplied no nav links gets no nav links.
 *   - THE HEADER CTA (navCtaText / navCtaHref) — decision 1. Those two fields
 *     are real schema paths, but this design's header has no button in it, and
 *     a hook is only honest if the pixel it edits exists.
 *   - contact.phone. It sits in the `header` schema group, but this design
 *     prints no phone on the rail; the visit block owns the number.
 *   - business_name / tagline. They drive <title> and the meta description,
 *     which PageBO owns. Nothing on this rail reads them except as the
 *     wordmark's fallback.
 *   - THE WHOLE RAIL, when brand, locality and links are all empty. A sticky
 *     bar with nothing in it is ~68px of dead chrome on every screen. It is the
 *     BODY content that is counted — never a tag or a headline, because
 *     lib/derive-content-defaults.ts fills those on every site and a gate that
 *     counted them would always pass. This rail has no tag and no headline at
 *     all, so the count is simply everything the rail can draw.
 *   - the disc, with no name: there is no initial to put in it.
 *   - the burger, unless there is a drawer for it to open.
 *   - a nav link with no label, or whose href reaches no section on this page.
 *     A 0-width tap target and a link that scrolls nowhere are both broken.
 *   Every guard sits OUTSIDE its tag, so a blank value removes the ELEMENT and
 *   not just its text — no empty purple disc, no lock with nothing in it.
 *   There is no star row, rating, review or numeral on this rail, so nothing
 *   here can print a claim the owner did not make. There is no upload either,
 *   so there is no role="img" and no .plate.
 *
 * DECISIONS A READER MIGHT OTHERWISE READ AS OVERSIGHTS
 *
 * 1. THERE IS NO BUTTON ON THIS RAIL, AND THAT IS THE DESIGN. All three
 *    siblings in this family (HeaderBL, HeaderBM, HeaderBN) carry a nav CTA
 *    because their designs draw one. Sipsip's header is a two-part row — lock
 *    left, pills right — and its call to action lives in the hero, on the cream
 *    button inside the purple plate. Dropping a --accent pill into the right
 *    end of this rail would change the composition the design specifies and
 *    put a second competing primary fill one scroll above the first. So
 *    navCtaText and navCtaHref are left unbound here. The consequence is stated
 *    plainly rather than hidden: an owner editing this template sees a
 *    "Header CTA" box in the sidebar that this page does not draw. That is a
 *    schema-wide field other templates own, and the alternative — inventing a
 *    control the design does not have so a sidebar row has somewhere to land —
 *    is the worse trade.
 *
 * 2. WHY THE TINT IS A PLAIN rgba() AND NOT A PSEUDO-ELEMENT. The design's rail
 *    is rgba(255,247,242,.94) — that is --paper at 94%. PageBO publishes
 *    --paper-rgb (and buildOverrideCss remaps it with the rest of the palette),
 *    so the design's exact value is expressible in one token declaration. No
 *    literal, no extra stacking context, same pixels.
 *
 * 3. NO HAIRLINE UNDER THE RAIL. HeaderBM draws one because its design does.
 *    This one does not: Sipsip separates the bar from the page with the blur
 *    and the 6% transparency alone. --line here resolves to the same #EEE2F5 as
 *    --lilac and --well, so a rule would in any case read as a lilac smudge
 *    rather than the crisp walnut hairline that template gets.
 *
 * 4. THE TOWN TAG IS NOT A .kick. The wrapper's .kick is 11px at .18em; the
 *    design's tag is 9px at .28em, stacked 1px under the wordmark. Those are
 *    different metrics, not a near-miss, so the tag is a scoped class at the
 *    design's own numbers. It takes the same --accent as .kick, which is what
 *    makes it read as part of this family.
 *
 * 5. WHY THREE LINE-HEIGHTS ARE RESTATED. The design declares line-height only
 *    on the wordmark (1); its disc, its tag and its nav pills render at the
 *    browser `normal` because the design's body sets no line-height. PageBO's
 *    body sets 1.72, so inheriting would put 13px nav labels in a 22.4px line
 *    box (13 x 1.72) and take the rail from ~68px to ~72px — 32px of padding
 *    around a 40.4px pill instead of a 36.3px lock — and would grow the 9px
 *    tag's line box from ~12.3px to 15.5px, dropping the tag ~1.6px (half the
 *    added leading lands above the glyphs) off the 1px gap the design puts
 *    under the wordmark. Each is therefore declared at the value the design
 *    actually renders: 1 on the disc, `normal` on the tag, 1.4 on the nav label
 *    (13 x 1.4 = 18.2px, a shade over the `normal` Nunito Sans gives at 13px).
 *    The rail then measures ~68px on desktop, and at most ~76px below 900px
 *    where the 44px burger sets the height. PageBO's scroll-padding-top: 92px
 *    clears both, so in-page anchors still land below the bar at every width.
 *
 * 6. WHY flex:none BECAME flex:0 1 auto ON THE LOCK. The design says flex:none,
 *    which at 360px lets a long shop name shove the burger off screen. The lock
 *    shrinks and the name wraps instead; at desktop widths the rendering is
 *    identical to the design. The DISC keeps flex:none — it is a circle, and a
 *    squashed circle is an ellipse.
 *
 * 7. THE NAME WRAPS; IT IS NOT ELLIPSISED. text-overflow needs overflow:hidden,
 *    and the design's line-height:1 makes the wordmark's clip box shorter than
 *    Baloo 2's own ascent-plus-descent — so an ellipsis here would shave the
 *    tails off the descenders in the owner's own name. Wrapping keeps the whole
 *    name legible, and overflow-wrap:anywhere means a long unbroken string
 *    breaks rather than pushing the page sideways. The one cost is that two
 *    wrapped lines at line-height 1 sit tight, which is why the 520 pass opens
 *    it to 1.1 (see the ladder note below — the LINE BOX still shrinks there).
 *
 * 8. THE BURGER AND DRAWER ARE AN ADDITION, NOT A RE-DERIVATION. The design is
 *    a desktop artboard; its nav is flex-wrap:wrap, which at 360px would stack
 *    five pills into a sticky block eating a third of the viewport on every
 *    scroll. So the row collapses into a drawer at one width (<=900px) — the
 *    row leaves and the burger arrives together, so there is never a band where
 *    neither is on screen. Nothing in the desktop rendering moves. The burger
 *    is drawn in this design's own vocabulary: a --lilac pill with --accent
 *    bars, which is exactly the wrapper's .chip pairing. --accent on --lilac
 *    computes to 4.77:1 (sRGB relative luminance, WCAG 2.x, from the hexes
 *    PageBO declares) — well past the 3:1 floor a non-text control needs. The
 *    drawer's ground is OPAQUE --paper, not --tint: --tint is invisible to the
 *    theme engine, so on a repainted scheme a --tint panel would hang over a
 *    page whose --paper had moved.
 *
 * 9. WHY THE NAV HOVER IS --accent-deep. It is the design's OWN hover: its
 *    stylesheet declares a:hover{color:#5C3580}, which is --accent-deep. In the
 *    demo that rule is shadowed by the inline colour on each nav link, so the
 *    designer's page happens to render no hover change — an artefact of
 *    inline-style specificity, not a decision. Shipping a five-link row with no
 *    hover state would be an interaction regression dressed up as fidelity.
 *    COMPUTED here, not estimated: --accent-deep on --paper is 8.72:1, over a
 *    rest colour of --ink-soft on --paper at 7.13:1 — sRGB relative luminance,
 *    WCAG 2.x, from the hexes PageBO declares. The 7.13 is the figure PageBO's
 *    own table gives; --accent-deep appears in no pair on that table, so the
 *    8.72 was computed here by the same method (which reproduces every figure
 *    the table does state).
 *
 * 10. NO CLAMP ANYWHERE IN THIS FILE, AND NO TYPE THAT GROWS. Every type value
 *     is a fixed px that only ever steps DOWN as the viewport narrows — disc
 *     36→32, disc letter 20→18, wordmark 23→20, tracking .28em→.22em, row gap
 *     20→12, row padding 16→13 — and the nav label holds the design's 13px at
 *     EVERY width. The one line-height that RISES, the wordmark's 1→1.1 at
 *     520px, lowers the value that matters: 23px x 1 = 23px becomes 20px x 1.1
 *     = 22px, so the line box shrinks with everything else.
 *     TWO THINGS DO GET BIGGER BELOW 900px, and both are TAP TARGETS rather
 *     than type — stated here so the rule above is not read as covering more
 *     than it does. The drawer row takes a 48px min-height and a 12/18 pad in
 *     place of the desktop 9/15, and the burger arrives at 44px square, which
 *     is what lifts the rail to ~76px there from the ~68px it measures on
 *     desktop. A touch target that shrank with the viewport would be the actual
 *     bug; nothing the reader reads moves. There are two breakpoints, 900 and
 *     520, and no third that could invert a floor under a cap the way an
 *     earlier audit found on another template.
 *
 * Never visibility-gated in this file: PageBO owns `visibility.headerSection`.
 * No .btn class appears here because there is no button (decision 1); the
 * forbidden .nav-cta / .nav-right / .btn-primary never come up.
 *
 * CLASS PREFIX: every scoped class is `sip-`, unique to this template. An
 * audit had to rename a whole set of classes copied verbatim from a sibling;
 * nothing here shares a name with HeaderBL's `hb-`, HeaderBM's `tcbm-` or
 * HeaderBN's `pcbn-`.
 */
interface Props { content: any; layout?: any; }
const { content, layout } = Astro.props;
const lay = (layout as any) || {};
const c = (content as any) || {};

// ── ANCHOR GUARD ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// lib/derive-content-defaults.ts hard-codes nav links to '#about', '#services',
// '#why', '#work', '#visit', and lib/astro-builder.ts falls back to '#about',
// '#services', '#gallery', '#contact' — ids most designs never render, so those
// links scrolled nowhere. Resolve every in-page href against what PageBO
// ACTUALLY renders: keep it if the id exists, remap it when this design carries
// the same section under another name, otherwise fall back (nav links pass ''
// and are dropped). Off-page hrefs — tel:, mailto:, http(s) and anything else
// without a leading '#' — pass through untouched.
const PAGE_ANCHORS = new Set(['menu', 'about', 'gallery', 'visit', 'faq', 'main']);
// This design renames five derived targets: the services block IS the drinks
// menu (rooms lands there too), '#about' is the shop's own story (host and why
// are the same block here), '#work' is the photo wall, and '#location' /
// '#contact' both mean the visit block — address, map and opening hours. A
// booking link has no checkout to reach on this page, so it lands on <main>.
const ANCHOR_ALIAS: Record<string, string> = {
  book: 'main',
  contact: 'visit',
  host: 'about',
  location: 'visit',
  rooms: 'menu',
  services: 'menu',
  why: 'about',
  work: 'gallery',
};
const onPage = (href: string | undefined, fallback: string): string => {
  if (!href) return fallback;
  if (!href.startsWith('#')) return href;
  const id = href.slice(1);
  if (PAGE_ANCHORS.has(id)) return href;
  const alias = ANCHOR_ALIAS[id];
  return alias && PAGE_ANCHORS.has(alias) ? `#${alias}` : fallback;
};

// The wordmark. 'Your Business' was a shipped placeholder that rendered as the
// customer's own name — with nothing supplied the mark is omitted instead.
// `||`, never `??`: genericContentSchema gives footer.brand
// fallbackPaths ['business_name'] and ContentFieldsAuto's readWithFallbacks
// treats '' as empty, so a CLEARED brand box still shows the business name in
// the sidebar. With `??` an empty string is a value, and the page would have
// gone wordmark-less while the sidebar displayed a name.
const brand = String(c.footer?.brand || lay.businessName || '').trim();
// The design's disc letter. Derived from the name above, never authored, and
// gone the moment the name is.
const initial = (Array.from(brand)[0] || '').toUpperCase();
// The town tag. One field, three consumers (here, the footer wordmark and the
// map caption) and nothing derives it, so on a first build it is simply absent
// rather than guessed out of the address.
const locality = String(c.footer?.locality ?? '').trim();

// Nav links. content.navbar_links is read FIRST and layout.navLinks only as a
// fallback: astro-builder copies the former onto the latter when it exists, but
// substitutes a four-row default of its own when it does not — and those rows
// are not the array the sidebar list edits. Reading content first guarantees
// that `i` indexes the array an edit actually writes into. `i` is the ORIGINAL
// index so per-link edits stay aimed at the row the owner clicked even after
// unreachable rows are dropped.
const rawNav: any[] = Array.isArray(c.navbar_links)
  ? c.navbar_links
  : (Array.isArray(lay.navLinks) ? lay.navLinks : []);
const navLinks: Array<{ href: string; label: string; i: number }> = rawNav
  .map((l: any, i: number) => ({ label: String(l?.label ?? '').trim(), href: onPage(l?.href, ''), i }))
  .filter((l: { href: string; label: string }) => l.href && l.label);

// The rail renders only when it CARRIES something — the lock or the links. Both
// counted values are body content; there is no tag or headline on this rail for
// a derived default to fake the gate with.
const hasRail = Boolean(brand || locality || navLinks.length > 0);
---
{hasRail && (
  <header class="sip-rail">
    <div class="wrap sip-in">
      {(brand || locality) && (
        <a class="sip-lock" href="#main">
          {initial && <span class="sip-seal" aria-hidden="true">{initial}</span>}
          <span class="sip-names">
            {brand && <span class="sip-mark" data-field="footer.brand">{brand}</span>}
            {locality && <span class="sip-town" data-field="footer.locality">{locality}</span>}
          </span>
        </a>
      )}
      {navLinks.length > 0 && (
        <nav class="sip-nav" id="sip-nav-BO" aria-label="Primary">
          {navLinks.map((link) => (
            <a
              href={link.href}
              data-field={`navbar_links.${link.i}.label`}
              data-href-field={`navbar_links.${link.i}.href`}
            >{link.label}</a>
          ))}
        </nav>
      )}
      {navLinks.length > 0 && (
        <button class="sip-burger" type="button" aria-label="Menu" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="sip-nav-BO">
          <span aria-hidden="true"></span><span aria-hidden="true"></span><span aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </button>
      )}
    </div>
  </header>
)}
<script is:inline>
  (function () {
    // Null-guarded rather than conditionally rendered: an is:inline script must
    // stay at the top level, and with no rail (or no drawer) it simply exits.
    // 'sip-rail' is template-unique because a preview harness can render two
    // wrappers into one document and a shared class cross-wires the drawers.
    var rail = document.querySelector('header.sip-rail');
    if (!rail) return;
    var btn = rail.querySelector('.sip-burger');
    if (!btn) return;
    function setOpen(open) {
      rail.classList.toggle('is-open', open);
      btn.setAttribute('aria-expanded', open ? 'true' : 'false');
    }
    btn.addEventListener('click', function () { setOpen(!rail.classList.contains('is-open')); });
    rail.querySelectorAll('.sip-nav a').forEach(function (a) {
      a.addEventListener('click', function () { setOpen(false); });
    });
    document.addEventListener('keydown', function (e) { if (e.key === 'Escape') setOpen(false); });
  })();
</script>
<style>
  /* The design: sticky, --paper at 94%, blur(14px), and NO hairline (decision
     3). The tint is a straight rgba() on --paper-rgb, which PageBO publishes
     and the theme engine remaps with the rest of the palette — so this is the
     design's own rgba(255,247,242,.94) with no literal in it.
     (Never write a literal end-style tag inside this block, comments included:
      it is an HTML raw-text element and the parser would end the stylesheet
      right there, silently truncating the file.) */
  .sip-rail {
    /* z-index is the design's own 50. The wrapper's skip link sits at 100, so
       the rail clears the page and the skip link still clears the rail. */
    position: sticky; top: 0; z-index: 50;
    background-color: rgba(var(--paper-rgb), .94);
    -webkit-backdrop-filter: blur(14px);
    backdrop-filter: blur(14px);
  }

  /* The design's row: 16px top and bottom, 20px gap, two parts spread apart.
     Its 28px side padding is the .wrap's job in this family (min(1200px,92vw)),
     so only the vertical value is restated here.
     NOT position:relative — the <=900px drawer is absolutely positioned and
     resolves against .sip-rail (position:sticky is a positioned element) so it
     spans the full rail width rather than the wrap. */
  .sip-in {
    display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 20px; padding: 16px 0;
  }

  /* flex:0 1 auto rather than the design's flex:none — decision 6. */
  .sip-lock {
    display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 10px;
    flex: 0 1 auto; min-width: 0; color: var(--ink);
  }
  /* The disc. flex:none is kept verbatim: a circle that shrinks is an ellipse.
     --on-accent on --accent is 5.63:1 by PageBO's own computed table. */
  .sip-seal {
    flex: none; width: 36px; height: 36px; border-radius: 50%;
    display: inline-flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;
    background-color: var(--accent); color: var(--on-accent);
    font-family: var(--disp); font-weight: 400; font-size: 20px; line-height: 1;
  }
  .sip-names { min-width: 0; }
  /* line-height is the DESIGN'S own 1. It is safe to keep tight here precisely
     because nothing clips it — see decision 7. NO letter-spacing: the design
     declares none on the wordmark, and the wrapper's -.01em reaches only
     h1-h4 / .h2, which this span is neither of. Weight 400 is likewise the
     design's — its wordmark sets no font-weight, so it renders at Baloo 2's
     regular, and stating it here keeps a stray inherited weight off it. */
  .sip-mark {
    display: block;
    font-family: var(--disp); font-weight: 400; font-size: 23px; line-height: 1;
    color: var(--ink); overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  /* NOT .kick — decision 4. 9px caps at .28em in purple, weight 700, sitting
     1px under the wordmark, and nothing else. */
  .sip-town {
    display: block; margin-top: 1px;
    font-family: var(--sans); font-weight: 700; font-size: 9px; line-height: normal;
    letter-spacing: .28em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--accent);
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* The design's nav: a wrapping row of pills at a 6px gap. margin-left:auto is
     an addition for the degenerate case the design never drew — a shop with no
     name and no town tag, where space-between with a single child would park
     the pills hard LEFT. With both parts present an auto margin eats the free
     space before justify-content sees it, so the desktop rendering is
     unchanged. */
  .sip-nav {
    display: flex; align-items: center; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 6px;
    margin-left: auto; min-width: 0;
  }
  .sip-nav a {
    display: inline-flex; align-items: center;
    font-family: var(--sans); font-weight: 600; font-size: 13px; line-height: 1.4;
    color: var(--ink-soft); padding: 9px 15px; border-radius: var(--r-pill);
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
    transition: color .18s ease;
  }
  /* The design's own hover, straight from its stylesheet: #5C3580, which is
     --accent-deep — decision 9. */
  .sip-nav a:hover { color: var(--accent-deep); }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) { .sip-nav a { transition: none; } }

  /* The design specifies no mobile rail (decision 8). The burger borrows this
     design's OWN secondary treatment — a --lilac pill with --accent marks,
     which is exactly the wrapper's .chip — so the addition stays inside the
     template's vocabulary and nothing on it has a sharp corner. */
  .sip-burger {
    display: none; flex: none; flex-direction: column; justify-content: center; gap: 5px;
    width: 44px; height: 44px; padding: 12px;
    background-color: var(--lilac); border: 0; border-radius: var(--r-pill);
    cursor: pointer;
  }
  .sip-burger span {
    display: block; width: 100%; height: 2px; border-radius: var(--r-pill);
    background-color: var(--accent);
    transition: transform .2s ease, opacity .2s ease;
  }
  /* 7px is bar height 2 + gap 5 — the exact centre-to-centre distance, so the
     two surviving bars meet on the middle one's axis. */
  header.sip-rail.is-open .sip-burger span:nth-child(1) { transform: translateY(7px) rotate(45deg); }
  header.sip-rail.is-open .sip-burger span:nth-child(2) { opacity: 0; }
  header.sip-rail.is-open .sip-burger span:nth-child(3) { transform: translateY(-7px) rotate(-45deg); }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) { .sip-burger span { transition: none; } }

  /* ONE collapse width: the row leaves and the burger arrives together, so
     there is never a band where neither is on screen. The drawer is OPAQUE
     --paper rather than the rail's 94% — it hangs over live page content and a
     translucent panel would print the page through the links — and it is told
     apart from the page by a soft shadow and a generous bottom radius, which is
     how this design draws every other boundary on it. */
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .sip-burger { display: flex; }
    .sip-nav {
      position: absolute; left: 0; right: 0; top: 100%;
      display: none; flex-direction: column; align-items: stretch;
      flex-wrap: nowrap; gap: 2px; margin-left: 0;
      background-color: var(--paper);
      border-radius: 0 0 var(--r-lg) var(--r-lg);
      box-shadow: 0 14px 30px rgba(var(--ink-rgb), .12);
      padding: 8px 16px 14px; max-height: 78vh; overflow-y: auto;
    }
    header.sip-rail.is-open .sip-nav { display: flex; }
    /* The ROW becomes a 48px target — min-height 48, pad 9/15 -> 12/18 — and
       the TYPE stays at the design's 13px. The target is bought with the row,
       never by growing type as the viewport narrows (decision 10). */
    .sip-nav a { display: flex; align-items: center; min-height: 48px; padding: 12px 18px; }
  }

  /* Everything here steps DOWN. The wordmark's line-height rises 1 -> 1.1, and
     that is deliberate: 23 x 1 = 23px becomes 20 x 1.1 = 22px, so the line box
     shrinks with the rest and two wrapped lines stop touching. */
  @media (max-width: 520px) {
    .sip-in { gap: 12px; padding: 13px 0; }
    .sip-seal { width: 32px; height: 32px; font-size: 18px; }
    .sip-mark { font-size: 20px; line-height: 1.1; }
    .sip-town { letter-spacing: .22em; }
  }
</style>
